  "synthesis_conclusions": [
    "Most research on biophilic design and human health used natural images, presence of plants, and visual connections to nature in indoor environments.",
    "Biophilic design patterns and elements (e.g., natural images, plants, visual/non-visual connections to nature, natural materials) improve human health by reducing psychological and physical stress, restoring attention, and enhancing positive emotions and aesthetic attraction.",
    "Biophilic design principles support human adaptation, engagement, and immersion in nature, leading to increased productivity, aesthetic appeal, emotional attachment, and physical and social benefits in built environments.",
    "The findings align with Attention Restoration Theory, Stress Reduction Theory, and concepts of landscape preference and restorativeness."
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "Lack of empirical studies on the health benefits of non-rhythmic sensory stimuli, thermal and airflow variability, presence of water, organized/complexity, and integrating parts to create a whole.",
    "Further research is needed on the benefits of actual exposure to landscapes and water for psychological and physiological health.",
    "Future studies could investigate micro-climate indicators in built environments and their influence on human feelings.",
    "Further investigation is warranted into how different forms of landscapes (e.g., English, French, Japanese, Chinese gardens), their organization, complexity, and pattern integration affect human health."
  ],
